\begin_layout Abstract
Snell-Descartes refraction law has still not a clear description in absorbing
 materials.
 Today, understanding transient optics involving intense laser irradiation
 and excitation of Surface Plasmon Polaritons under intense fields require
 a proper description of the refraction angle in absorbing materials, also
 while the approximation of low-absorbing materials drops down.
 In this work, several available models are compared with FDTD 3D simulations
 in absorbing materials and with ellipsometric phase measurements in reflection
 configuration.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset toc
LatexCommand tableofcontents

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite that the problem of refraction law in absorbing materials is treated
 in many references from literature 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Born1980,Palik1985,Kovalenko2001,Shen2010"

\end_inset

, no satisfying description was available in the case of strongly absorbing
 materials so far.
 Moreover, it is common to find references in which this difficulty is not
 mentioned despite that a proper description of refraction in strongly absorbing
 materials was required in both numerical 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Bulgakova2010"

\end_inset

 and experimental studies 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Sokolowski-Tinten2000,Shank1983"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The well-known law for refraction is given by Snell-Descartes law 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\theta_{i+1}=\arcsin\left(\sqrt{\frac{\varepsilon_{i}}{\varepsilon_{i+1}}}\sin\theta_{i}\right).
\]

\end_inset

Snell-Descartes law must be valid in case of complex dielectric functions
 as stated by Stratton 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Stratton1941"

\end_inset

 (and experimentally proved by ???).
 In case of total reflection, a phase shift between equiphase plans and
 constant amplitude plans appears, leading to heterogeneous waves, then
 to interferences.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This work aims to correctly describe the refraction angle as a function
 of the incident angle and validate a general multilayer model, valid in
 both transparent and absorbing media.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At the interface with a slab material, a monochromatic wave experiments
 a reflection and a refraction as well described by the well-known Snell-Descart
es law as follows for air environment 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sin\theta_{i}=n\sin\theta_{r},
\]

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i}\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 is the incident angle, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{r}$
\end_inset

 is the refracted angle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, as soon as we consider an absorbing material, then, the optical
 index (and thus the dielectric permittivity 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon=n^{2}$
\end_inset

) is a complex-valued quantity 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
n\in\mathbb{C}
\]

\end_inset

and the angles of refraction also become a complex-valued quantity.
 As a consequence, the Snell law can remains valid, but only under certain
 conditions and an extended physical picture has to be considered.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The meaning of a complex angle
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, a complex angle has not a clear physical meaning.
 In the outer region of the sample, the incidence angle is well defined
 and real-valued.
 As soon as the wave gets into the material, the angles (both refraction
 and reflection) become complex-valued.
 Two interpretations can be given to a complex angle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(i) Born and Wolf 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Born1980"

\end_inset

 considered it as a change of optical index as the angle is changing, which
 can be understood as anisotropy.
 However, nothing tells that simply using complex angles inside the sample
 is a sufficient formalism to properly consider this anisotropy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(ii) A physical picture was proposed describing a shift between the equal
 amplitude wave front and the equal phase front.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In both cases, the angle of refraction cannot be considered as a well-defined
 peak value anymore and becomes a large distribution.
 A change of angle of refraction is nothing else than a change of the wave
 momentum, which is actually a 
\emph on
scattering
\emph default
 
\emph on
event
\emph default
.
 The laser wave is scattered by matter and interacts (at optical frequencies)
 with the electronic band structure.
 The case of an absorbing material requires to consider a rigorous approach
 to describe properly the wave scattering by an absorbing sample, for example
 by using transmission matrix formulation or direct solving of Maxwell equations.
 Anisotropic solution are found and a vector field formulation might be
 unavoidable.
 Recently, time-dependent Maxwell calculations have been performed in order
 to correctly reproduce the energy-dependent reflectivity measurements 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Zhang2014b"

\end_inset

, and have identified a clear anisotropic response from the sample 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Bulgakova2013"

\end_inset

, giving explanation to arising anistropic structures such as nanogratings
 in silica.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
How to solve the problem ?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before considering any advanced theories, it is proposed to consider the
 Snell Law using a multi-layered reflection model to calculate the sample
 reflectivity of a stratified material.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By comparison with a FDTD Maxwell 3D calculation, it will be shown that
 Snell-Descartes law can remain valid for low absorbing materials, but fails
 in the case of strongly absorbing materials.
 A simple criterion identifying the validity limit of the complex-valued
 Snell-Descartes law will be also proposed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Could an experiment measure that ?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
About measuring the angle
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Not a simple one.
 Actually, the refracted angle can be measured if you can measure inside
 matter.
 Due to the backward light principle, the angle that is measured by reflection
 or by transmission through a thin-film is the same as the incident one,
 whatever happens inside the sample!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The way to produce such a measurement is thus to make the problem time-dependent
 and to observe a change of the refraction angle as a function of time !
 This can be performed by an intense and ultrashort laser pulse which changes
 the optical properties of the sample as a function of time, and observe
 the transmitted wave direction evolution (how ?).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
About measuring the reflected beam phase
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, by considering a multilayer model to calculate the surface reflectivity
, it would be possible to obtain the phase of the reflected signal theoretically.
 The formula involves the transmission angles inside the material, and complex
 optical indices.
 As a consequence, it is possible to compare the phase of the reflected
 beam with the phase of the calculated one.
 A comparison with FDTD calculation is also possible, despite that I focused
 on obtained the angle instead of the phase of the reflected beam.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
So, is it so bad, doctor ?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So it seems that this theoretical limit of the Snell law is a real physical
 problem, and that the concept of refractive angle inside matter is not
 well understood yet for any materials.
 However, in the theoretical world (so in simulation codes!), this angle
 is required, and is still a complex-valued quantity.
 So this problem is important and must be solved in a clear way.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Theoretical limit of Snell-Descartes law: mathematical and physical reasons
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let's investigate now why Snell-Descartes low is limited to low-absorbing
 materials.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Snell law is expressed by the following formula, using the dielectric
 permittivity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i}}\sin\theta_{i}=\sqrt{\varepsilon_{i+1}}\sin\theta_{i+1}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is the i-th layer of the sample.
 The complex-valued refractive angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i+1}$
\end_inset

 is given by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\theta_{i+1}=\sin^{-1}\left[\sin\theta_{i}\sqrt{\frac{\varepsilon_{i}}{\varepsilon_{i+1}}}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The real-valued 
\begin_inset Formula $\arcsin$
\end_inset

 function applied to 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 has the following property 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|\arcsin(x)\right|\le\frac{\pi}{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, the complex-valued number 
\begin_inset Formula $\arcsin(z)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

 has a modulus which looses its trigonometric properties as soon as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|\sqrt{\frac{\varepsilon_{i}}{\varepsilon_{i+1}}}\right|>1
\]

\end_inset

This situation actually leads to a loss of the angle definition and constitutes
 a criterion which allows to check if the Snell-Descartes law can give an
 accurate description in an absorbing medium.
 This is the mathematical reason.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The physical reason is that the angle of refraction does not stand any longer
 since scattering occurs and that the angle becomes a broad distribution,
 which should be taken into account to conserve the energy and momentum.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
What if I just take the real part of the angle ?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let's do it.
 If we consider to take the real part of the ratio 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Re e\sqrt{\frac{\varepsilon_{i}}{\varepsilon_{i+1}}},
\]

\end_inset

then, indeed, the modulus of the sinus content is limited to the unity,
 and then no mathematical problem emerges from the model.
 However, this description is clearly not physical and is not valid in absorbing
 materials as demonstrated in the next section.
 
\end_layout

